WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:04.000
Eh… the island of Pabbay now is just due north of Berneray,

00:04.000 --> 00:12.000
probably five or six hundred years ago these islands,

00:12.000 --> 00:15.000
probably Pabbay and Boreray were joined together.

00:17.000 --> 00:19.000
And there was nothing but sand. 

00:20.000 --> 00:24.000
And erosion is one of the problems on this island here. 

00:25.000 --> 00:34.000
But, I think it was the beginning of the 17th century, 1700,

00:37.000 --> 00:42.000
there was a horrific gale from the north,

00:42.000 --> 00:45.000
and it was said that three hundred,

00:46.000 --> 00:52.000
three hundred acres of land was blown away with the wind.

00:53.000 --> 00:56.000
That is how severe it was.

00:56.000 --> 01:01.000
And even from that point up there called Corran,

01:01.000 --> 01:04.000
which is, you know, more or less a point,

01:06.000 --> 01:11.000
it almost buried houses down in Siabaidh

01:11.000 --> 01:14.000
that’s where, the south end of the island.

01:15.000 --> 01:19.000
And, but going back to Pabbay,

01:19.000 --> 01:23.000
it’s now, it’s probably a mile in between them,

01:23.000 --> 01:27.000
whereas, at one time, when the womenfolk were washing,

01:27.000 --> 01:32.000
they could throw the washing bat they had in those days,

01:32.000 --> 01:34.000
like a cricket bat.

01:35.000 --> 01:39.000
And they used to throw it from Pabbay to Berneray.

01:40.000 --> 01:43.000
But in the, then they started,

01:43.000 --> 01:49.000
it’s a very fertile island, and facing south and getting a lot of sun.

01:49.000 --> 01:51.000
If there’s sun at all, it gets it. 

01:52.000 --> 01:55.000
They started distilling liquor 

01:56.000 --> 01:59.000
and they used to call it ‘the blend’.

01:59.000 --> 02:07.000
And in 1842, they were evicted, solely for the liquor– for the…

02:08.000 --> 02:11.000
distilling of liquor. 

02:13.000 --> 02:18.000
Now, before then though, the…

02:19.000 --> 02:22.000
what do you call them?

02:22.000 --> 02:26.000
The excisemen were trying to get to them, but they couldn’t.

02:27.000 --> 02:30.000
Well, eventually they did but…

02:31.000 --> 02:34.000
everytime the excisemen went out to Pabbay,

02:35.000 --> 02:39.000
it was always a boat from Berneray here they summoned. 

02:41.000 --> 02:47.000
And there was a sign if there was an exciseman aboard.

02:47.000 --> 02:51.000
Now this is how I heard the stories from my father and back…

02:53.000 --> 02:59.000
Even though it was a beautiful day, the sail was rigged one down

02:59.000 --> 03:02.000
so they could see the mast quite distinctively.

03:04.000 --> 03:07.000
And this happened, and if they, if the people,

03:07.000 --> 03:10.000
when they saw the mast like this,

03:10.000 --> 03:12.000
‘ah ah, there’s somebody aboard.’

03:12.000 --> 03:16.000
By the time the boat… everything was away, hidden. 

03:17.000 --> 03:19.000
But this time, for some unknown reason,

03:19.000 --> 03:23.000
there was a boat from Leverburgh, 

03:24.000 --> 03:35.000
and, the dear man, he didn’t know that this sign call was 

03:36.000 --> 03:39.000
evidence that there was somebody aboard.

03:39.000 --> 03:42.000
And he went there in the full sail.

03:42.000 --> 03:45.000
Now the children, there was always the children meeting the boats,

03:46.000 --> 03:53.000
and it was the eldest or the older children 

03:54.000 --> 03:56.000
that recognised the face,

03:56.000 --> 03:59.000
or the faces, of the excisemen.

04:00.000 --> 04:04.000
And they told the younger children: 

04:04.000 --> 04:09.000
“Go home, do your stuff, tell them the exciseman–

04:09.000 --> 04:11.000
tell them who are here.”

04:12.000 --> 04:18.000
But it was my great, great grandfather,

04:18.000 --> 04:23.000
and he wasn’t young at that time,

04:23.000 --> 04:27.000
he got an empty barrel and he placed it on his shoulders,

04:27.000 --> 04:30.000
between his shoulder blades,

04:30.000 --> 04:33.000
a man called Donnchadh mac Dhòmhnaill ’ic Alasdair.

04:35.000 --> 04:38.000
And he made for the top of the hill.

04:40.000 --> 04:42.000
This was sort of, to lure,

04:42.000 --> 04:45.000
he was more or less acting as the pied piper of Hamelin,

04:45.000 --> 04:51.000
trying to take the, what do you call them,

04:51.000 --> 04:54.000
the excisemen away, to lure them away.

04:54.000 --> 04:56.000
And he made for the top of the hill.

04:56.000 --> 04:59.000
Now, Pabbay, on the south side it’s very very,

04:59.000 --> 05:01.000
it’s not so steep.

05:01.000 --> 05:08.000
But it’s very steep on the back, this is the northerly slopes.

05:08.000 --> 05:11.000
So he knew that when he got to the top,

05:11.000 --> 05:13.000
that all he had to do was push the barrel 

05:13.000 --> 05:16.000
and the barrel would head towards the sea.

05:16.000 --> 05:19.000
It didn’t go far there when the barrel, sort of,

05:19.000 --> 05:22.000
went over a cliff and got broken.

05:25.000 --> 05:31.000
By the time they got home, everything was hidden away.

05:32.000 --> 05:41.000
But that was the end of people in Pabbay,

05:41.000 --> 05:47.000
because the excisemen knew there was some hanky-panky going

05:48.000 --> 05:51.000
and that was the end of time for them.

05:52.000 --> 05:53.000
And that’s when they got evicted?

05:53.000 --> 05:56.000
That’s when they– after that they were evicted, they were,

05:56.000 --> 06:02.000
I think it was 1840, 1842 the people left Pabbay.

06:03.000 --> 06:07.000
Some came here to Berneray, some went to the west coast of Harris…

06:10.000 --> 06:12.000
I think to, and to Scalpay. 

06:14.000 --> 06:16.000
But just like the people of St Kilda,

06:18.000 --> 06:24.000
they had to leave and they, lovely too, it’s a beautiful island.

06:24.000 --> 06:26.000
Lovely lovely island. And so is Boreray.

06:26.000 --> 06:29.000
Boreray is a very nice island too. 

06:29.000 --> 06:34.000
Both are very sandy soil so that’s–

06:34.000 --> 06:43.000
the people of Pabbay, and incidentally we had a girl here

06:43.000 --> 06:53.000
from Sydney in Australia this week, called Janet MacLeod, 

06:53.000 --> 06:58.000
and her great-grandmother was born in Pabbay.

06:59.000 --> 07:05.000
She wanted to get there but the weather wasn’t suitable,

07:05.000 --> 07:07.000
but she’s coming back. She’s coming back.

07:10.000 --> 07:12.000
So there goes, there goes.

07:14.000 --> 07:15.000
[BREAK IN TAPE]

07:16.000 --> 07:22.000
My father told me this story, well we knew a bit about him,

07:22.000 --> 07:28.000
but this gentleman William Stewart from Strond in Leverburgh,

07:28.000 --> 07:33.000
and he was a sanitary inspector for the council.

07:34.000 --> 07:39.000
And every time he came over to Berneray, or to the Uists,

07:39.000 --> 07:44.000
he always stayed with my mother and father.

07:44.000 --> 07:51.000
And, the daughter, Peigi-Ann is still living in Inverness 

07:51.000 --> 07:58.000
and I remember, as a wee boy, having breakfast with Mr Stewart, 

07:58.000 --> 08:00.000
with a white beard.

08:01.000 --> 08:03.000
An elegant looking man.

08:03.000 --> 08:07.000
And he loved porridge.

08:07.000 --> 08:11.000
And that’s the first time I’d ever seen anybody having 

08:11.000 --> 08:18.000
treacle on porridge and it’s, I have it,

08:18.000 --> 08:20.000
I still have it, I still have it –

08:20.000 --> 08:21.000
it’s good for you. 

08:22.000 --> 08:28.000
But he had somebody with him, I think from the Scottish Office,

08:30.000 --> 08:33.000
and they were going up to the top of the hill here,

08:33.000 --> 08:38.000
Beinn a’ Chlaidh, now that’s Hill of the Cemetery.

08:40.000 --> 08:45.000
And he asked my father for a spade, for the loan of a spade.

08:45.000 --> 08:50.000
My father gave it to him and when they came back he…

08:51.000 --> 08:56.000
said to my father, this gentleman, the other gentleman,

08:56.000 --> 09:02.000
he asked me to… he measured a few steps this way and that way,

09:02.000 --> 09:04.000
and he says: “Dig there.”

09:04.000 --> 09:08.000
And just about a foot down there was a black stone

09:08.000 --> 09:12.000
about thirty inches with a hollow and a…

09:12.000 --> 09:15.000
and this man told my father, he says:

09:15.000 --> 09:21.000
“You could almost swear that the blood was still on the stone.”

09:21.000 --> 09:26.000
Now, that stone, to follow on with the story.

09:26.000 --> 09:29.000
If in those days, like sheep-stealing,

09:29.000 --> 09:31.000
you could be beheaded.

09:32.000 --> 09:37.000
And there was no gallows here, and that’s apparently what they did.

09:37.000 --> 09:44.000
But there’s two, due north of the burn side here,

09:44.000 --> 09:46.000
or of the village hall nowadays,

09:47.000 --> 09:50.000
there’s two stone chairs.

09:50.000 --> 09:58.000
And… I think they were classed as judgement seats and…

09:59.000 --> 10:08.000
they were placed in the seat and when the sun cast his

10:08.000 --> 10:11.000
head’s shadow on the top of Pabbay, 

10:12.000 --> 10:16.000
that’s when the verdict was brought up.

10:17.000 --> 10:22.000
And it would be twelve noon, exactly about twelve noon,

10:23.000 --> 10:27.000
And if the verdict was guilty, 

10:27.000 --> 10:30.000
he was taken up to this hill here,

10:30.000 --> 10:33.000
Beinn a’ Chlaidh, and beheaded. 

10:34.000 --> 10:39.000
And the body, or the torso, was buried there.

10:40.000 --> 10:46.000
And due west of the hill, down in Siabaidh,

10:46.000 --> 10:47.000
the south end of the island,

10:47.000 --> 10:53.000
there’s a wee knoll, or a hillock, and it’s called Cnoc nan Claigeann.

10:54.000 --> 10:56.000
Now ‘cnoc’ is a hillock,

10:56.000 --> 10:58.000
and ‘claigeann’ is the skull.

10:58.000 --> 11:03.000
So it’s, it could be translated as ‘hill’, or ‘hillock of the skulls’.

11:04.000 --> 11:09.000
And Kenny Morrison from next door,

11:09.000 --> 11:16.000
he told me that he and John Ferguson one day started digging

11:16.000 --> 11:19.000
and they did come upon skulls.

11:20.000 --> 11:25.000
Now, looking at it from etiher side it’s just,

11:27.000 --> 11:35.000
like a round mound and, probably, it is man-made.

11:37.000 --> 11:44.000
It wouldn’t be so exact on every side if it was left to…

11:44.000 --> 11:49.000
well, if it was a natural thing.

11:49.000 --> 11:53.000
But it very much looks like being man-made.

11:54.000 --> 12:01.000
So that’s the story, and that’s Cnoc nan Claigeann.

12:01.000 --> 12:03.000
Cnoc nan Claigeann.

12:03.000 --> 12:07.000
And to this day it’s… still holds that name.

12:07.000 --> 12:08.000
Very good.

12:08.000 --> 12:14.000
Now, the cemetery, the cemetery up there in Beinn a’ Chlaidh,

12:15.000 --> 12:19.000
there is a standing stone like the stones in Callanish

12:19.000 --> 12:21.000
or you see them ocassionaly.

12:22.000 --> 12:25.000
And it’s about ten feet, it’s about a foot thick.

12:25.000 --> 12:27.000
Is that the one I was at today?

12:26.000 --> 12:27.000
Yes.

12:27.000 --> 12:28.000
Just up there?

12:28.000 --> 12:29.000
Up there, yes.

12:29.000 --> 12:30.000
There’s a cemetery up there?

12:30.000 --> 12:34.000
Yes, oh she’s right– that stone is in the middle of the cemetery,

12:34.000 --> 12:38.000
and you can see it faintly right round.

12:39.000 --> 12:44.000
And there was even a chapel, a chapel.

12:45.000 --> 12:51.000
And I remember Hector MacKinnon, the late Hector MacKinnon,

12:51.000 --> 12:54.000
Duncan’s – have you spoken to Duncan?

12:54.000 --> 12:56.000
No I haven’t spoken to Duncan.

12:56.000 --> 13:02.000
Oh, he’s… Duncan MacKinnon’s father, Hector Mackinnon.

13:02.000 --> 13:06.000
He was an elder in the church and, in between the services,

13:06.000 --> 13:11.000
and I remember that, just being very, very young, on Sundays,

13:11.000 --> 13:15.000
Hector MacKinnon having services – open-air services – there.

13:16.000 --> 13:20.000
That’s right, yes, aye. But there’s a cemetery right about it.

13:22.000 --> 13:23.000
[BREAK IN TAPE]

13:24.000 --> 13:26.000
It’s fascinating that, that they kept the stone up,

13:26.000 --> 13:28.000
d’ you know, because a lot of,

13:28.000 --> 13:31.000
a lot of stones were pushed down,

13:31.000 --> 13:33.000
standing stones and stone circles were pushed down.

13:33.000 --> 13:35.000
Eh, well, yes but it’s still, it’s still–

13:35.000 --> 13:37.000
yes, nobody seemed to touch it.

13:37.000 --> 13:40.000
But there’s– due west of that,

13:40.000 --> 13:44.000
probably about three to four hundred yards

13:44.000 --> 13:49.000
it is said that there is four, at least four,

13:49.000 --> 13:52.000
kings buried down there.

13:52.000 --> 13:54.000
The Lord of the Isles?

13:54.000 --> 13:59.000
I’m… goodness knows, I’m not a great historian at all but…

14:00.000 --> 14:06.000
because, well it’s called Lag mo Rìgh:

14:06.000 --> 14:11.000
‘Lag’ is a hollow, and ‘rìgh’ is the king so it’s…

14:12.000 --> 14:24.000
phonetically translating it, it’s the… how will I say it? 

14:24.000 --> 14:26.000
The Hollow of the King. 

14:27.000 --> 14:31.000
Now, quite near, on the north side of the point

14:31.000 --> 14:38.000
where the ferry terminal is, well actually it is there,

14:38.000 --> 14:42.000
that’s where the ferry terminal is, it’s ‘Àird mo Rìgh’.

14:42.000 --> 14:44.000
‘Àird’ is a point.

14:44.000 --> 14:46.000
King’s Point.

14:46.000 --> 14:50.000
Yes, yes, the Point of the King.

14:51.000 --> 14:57.000
Now whether a king landed there or whatever, that is,

14:57.000 --> 15:00.000
it’s to this day ‘Lag– Àird mo Rìgh’.

15:02.000 --> 15:06.000
So there must be something, some connection,

15:06.000 --> 15:11.000
but supposed to be four kings buried there.

15:13.000 --> 15:16.000
And then if you just carry on going around the island,

15:16.000 --> 15:19.000
along the machair there, when I was here last summer

15:19.000 --> 15:24.000
with Donald MacRury, he took me over to the MacAskills’ Monument.

15:24.000 --> 15:28.000
But then, just left of there there are 

15:28.000 --> 15:32.000
some old building structures [tape trips] An old village?

15:32.000 --> 15:39.000
Well yes, there was a village there and it was in,

15:39.000 --> 15:47.000
was it 1665 or 1670 when this horrific gale from the north

15:47.000 --> 15:52.000
blew the sand and it almost buried them down in Siabaidh there.

15:52.000 --> 15:56.000
Their houses was almost covered with sand because

15:56.000 --> 15:59.000
apparently, as I said, three hundred–

15:59.000 --> 16:02.000
approximately three hundred acres were blown away.

16:03.000 --> 16:04.000
That’s what happened because

16:04.000 --> 16:06.000
I knew something happened there.

16:05.000 --> 16:06.000
Yes, that’s right.

16:06.000 --> 16:08.000
Uhuh, very good.

16:08.000 --> 16:10.000
So…

16:11.000 --> 16:12.000
[BREAK IN TAPE]

16:12.000 --> 16:14.000
Caisteal a’ Ghlinne.

16:15.000 --> 16:20.000
Chuir iad do Chaisteal a’ Ghlinne mi 

16:20.000 --> 16:25.000
An t-seòmbar ìosal cho fuar;

16:26.000 --> 16:30.000
Chàraich iad mis’ ann am prìosan

16:30.000 --> 16:35.000
’S an fhìrinn agam ga luaidh;

16:36.000 --> 16:40.000
Creideas chan fhaighinn no èisteachd

16:40.000 --> 16:45.000
Don sgeul a dh’fhàg mi fo ghruaim;

16:45.000 --> 16:50.000
’S ged dèanadh iad mise a cheusadh,

16:51.000 --> 16:56.000
Chan èirich is’ às an uaigh.

16:57.000 --> 17:02.000
Cadal cha tig air mo shùilean,

17:02.000 --> 17:07.000
Gur tùrsach m’ aigne gach là,

17:07.000 --> 17:13.000
’S mi cuimhneachadh maise mo rùin-sa

17:13.000 --> 17:18.000
’S a gnùis bha coibhneil a ghnàth;

17:18.000 --> 17:23.000
Cridhe bha blàth agus mùirneach

17:23.000 --> 17:28.000
Seinn ciùil mar uiseag na neòil,

17:28.000 --> 17:33.000
Is truagh nach robh sinn le chèile 

17:33.000 --> 17:39.000
’S mi ’g èisteachd brìodal a beòil.

17:40.000 --> 17:45.000
Ach thusa cha tig ann am ionnsaigh

17:45.000 --> 17:51.000
’S cha dùisg à cadal a’ bhàis;

17:51.000 --> 17:56.000
Ach tachraidh mise rim rùin-sa 

17:56.000 --> 18:01.000
An dùthaich bheannaicht’ an àigh,

18:01.000 --> 18:07.000
Mo leannan bheir maitheanas dhomhsa 

18:07.000 --> 18:13.000
Ged leòn mi ise gu bàs,

18:13.000 --> 18:18.000
Crìoch cha tig air ar sòlas,

18:18.000 --> 18:24.000
Is leòn cha chluinnear gu bràth.

18:25.000 --> 18:26.000
[BREAK IN TAPE]

18:26.000 --> 18:31.000
Guma slàn a chì mi, 

18:31.000 --> 18:35.000
Mo chailin dìleas donn;

18:35.000 --> 18:39.000
Bean a’ chuailein rèidh 

18:39.000 --> 18:44.000
Air an deise a dh’èireadh fonn

18:44.000 --> 18:49.000
’S i cainnt do bheòil bu bhinne leam 

18:49.000 --> 18:53.000
An uair a bhiodh m’ inntinn trom,

18:53.000 --> 18:58.000
’S tu thogadh suas mo chridhe-sa

18:58.000 --> 19:03.000
Nuair a bhiodh tu bruidhinn rium.

19:04.000 --> 19:09.000
Tacan mun do sheòl sinn

19:09.000 --> 19:14.000
’S ann a thòisich càch

19:14.000 --> 19:18.000
Ri innseadh do mo chruinneig-sa 

19:18.000 --> 19:23.000
Nach tillinn-sa gu bràth,

19:23.000 --> 19:28.000
Na cuireadh siud ort cùram, 

19:28.000 --> 19:33.000
A rùin, ma bhios mi slàn;

19:33.000 --> 19:38.000
Nach cùm dad idir bhuat mi 

19:38.000 --> 19:43.000
Ach saighead cruaidh a’ bhàis.

19:45.000 --> 19:51.000
’S gur muladach a tha mi

19:51.000 --> 19:55.000
’S mi nochd air àird a’ chuain,

19:55.000 --> 20:00.000
Neo-shunndach mo chadal dhomh,

20:00.000 --> 20:05.000
’S do chaidreabh fada bhuam;

20:05.000 --> 20:10.000
Gur tric mi ort a’ smaointinn, 

20:10.000 --> 20:15.000
Às d’ eugmhais tha mi truagh;

20:15.000 --> 20:20.000
Is mura dèan mi d’ fhaotainn,

20:20.000 --> 20:26.000
Cha bhi mo shaoghal buan.

20:26.000 --> 20:27.000
Seo a-nist’.

20:27.000 --> 20:29.000
Very good. What’s it called?

20:30.000 --> 20:42.000
Guma slàn a… c, h… a chì mi.

20:42.000 --> 20:45.000
A chì mi, and what does that translate into?

20:45.000 --> 20:54.000
Guma slàn a chì mi… I hope everyday I see…

20:54.000 --> 21:01.000
I mean, well literally, that this girl is healthy everyday I see her.

21:02.000 --> 21:05.000
Guma slàn a chì mi mo chailin dìleas donn, pardon me.

21:05.000 --> 21:06.000
Aye.

21:07.000 --> 21:08.000
[BREAK IN TAPE]

21:08.000 --> 21:11.000
Right beside Donald MacKillop down there,

21:12.000 --> 21:16.000
there was a man called Kenneth Morrison

21:16.000 --> 21:24.000
and he was a shepherd, or probably a shepherd and a cowherd.

21:25.000 --> 21:31.000
But he used to go, leave Lochmaddy on the boat,

21:32.000 --> 21:35.000
go down to Glasgow and walk to Falkirk…

21:38.000 --> 21:40.000
with the animals, with the cattle.

21:41.000 --> 21:45.000
Because this end of Berneray, in those days,

21:45.000 --> 21:49.000
was what they called a ‘tack’. T-a-c-k.

21:49.000 --> 21:53.000
And tack, that is just like a, like a small estate,

21:55.000 --> 21:58.000
probably in this instance a miniature estate,

21:58.000 --> 22:00.000
but it’s a tack.

22:01.000 --> 22:08.000
And he had a very good dog called Clyde,

22:09.000 --> 22:14.000
and somehow in Falkirk, at the sales, he missed the dog.

22:15.000 --> 22:19.000
Did he have one too many? We don’t, I don’t know.

22:19.000 --> 22:24.000
But Kenneth did his way back to the… 

22:25.000 --> 22:30.000
to Glasgow and got the boat, but he lost the dog.

22:32.000 --> 22:35.000
Coinneach, or Kenneth, Coinneach is Gaelic for Kenneth,

22:35.000 --> 22:39.000
and he came home and…

22:40.000 --> 22:46.000
apparently this dog made his way to Glasgow and it was…

22:48.000 --> 22:52.000
Dun Ara Castle or one of these boats,

22:54.000 --> 22:57.000
the old Hebrides or whichever,

22:57.000 --> 23:03.000
this dog knew that ship, and jumped aboard.

23:03.000 --> 23:08.000
And the crew were trying to get the dog out.

23:08.000 --> 23:11.000
And there was no power this side of kingdom come 

23:11.000 --> 23:14.000
that would put the dog out of the, out of the ship. 

23:15.000 --> 23:19.000
And eventually, somehow, this came to the skippers ears and he said:

23:20.000 --> 23:23.000
“Leave the dog, and feed him.

23:23.000 --> 23:26.000
There must be a purpose for this dog.”

23:26.000 --> 23:33.000
So the dog got fed, watered, and each port they called,

23:34.000 --> 23:39.000
like all the Small Isles and probably Oban, the dog…

23:39.000 --> 23:40.000
and he was, by the way,

23:40.000 --> 23:45.000
he was sleeping in a coil of rope in the bowel.

23:47.000 --> 23:50.000
And each port they came to, he would sort of

23:50.000 --> 23:56.000
have a look and settle back down until he came to Lochmaddy,

23:56.000 --> 23:59.000
and one look and he was out.

24:00.000 --> 24:06.000
And he walked in to the place, Otternish nowadays,

24:06.000 --> 24:11.000
there’s Otternish and there’s another place right beside it

24:11.000 --> 24:16.000
called Leac Bhàn, ‘bàn’ is fair and 

24:16.000 --> 24:18.000
there’s two or three flagstones, you know,

24:18.000 --> 24:21.000
a rock formation and it’s called Leac Bhàn.

24:22.000 --> 24:26.000
And eh, quite near that there was the…

24:27.000 --> 24:29.000
but I’ll finish that story first.

24:30.000 --> 24:33.000
Coinneach and his mother were at home 

24:34.000 --> 24:38.000
and his mother happened to go outside and she said: 

24:38.000 --> 24:43.000
“Kenneth, if Clyde was alive, I would say

24:43.000 --> 24:46.000
that he’s barking on the other side.”

24:47.000 --> 24:51.000
And he said: “Oh woman, just have a bit of sense.”

24:52.000 --> 25:00.000
And they went in and apparently had their dinner, or a meal, and…

25:00.000 --> 25:05.000
the dog swam the channel there and came home.

25:05.000 --> 25:07.000
And that was Clyde. 

25:08.000 --> 25:13.000
But going back to Leac Bhàn there,

25:14.000 --> 25:17.000
that’s where the people came when they wanted 

25:17.000 --> 25:21.000
to be ferried across and he,

25:22.000 --> 25:27.000
I think Kenneth Morrison, in those days, did the ferrying,

25:27.000 --> 25:29.000
he had a rowing boat.

25:29.000 --> 25:31.000
And if you wanted home in a hurry,

25:32.000 --> 25:36.000
you would sit on a certain flagstone

25:37.000 --> 25:40.000
and that was called Leac an Tastain.

25:40.000 --> 25:44.000
You sat on this flagstone and you were willing to pay a shilling…

25:45.000 --> 25:49.000
but if you weren’t in any hurry or

25:50.000 --> 25:55.000
didn’t want to expend too much money,

25:55.000 --> 25:59.000
you sat on the sixpenny one, so that’s Leac an t-Sia Sgillinn.

26:00.000 --> 26:02.000
So that’s the story about…

26:02.000 --> 26:06.000
it’s different from nowadays when there’s two ferries there,

26:06.000 --> 26:08.000
one to Harris and one to Berneray.

26:08.000 --> 26:10.000
That’s good. I like the stories about the

26:10.000 --> 26:16.000
certain physical structures, the rocks or a certain causeway or…

26:16.000 --> 26:18.000
just, it’s really good.

26:18.000 --> 26:21.000
Oh aye and these two, these two flagstones…

26:21.000 --> 26:24.000
they were natural, they weren’t manmade at all,

26:24.000 --> 26:25.000
they were natural.

26:25.000 --> 26:31.000
And they were sort of, quite distinct. Distinctively apart.

26:32.000 --> 26:37.000
And if you were in a hurry, hungry or wet and cold

26:37.000 --> 26:44.000
and you were, sort of, willing to part with a shilling,

26:44.000 --> 26:46.000
you sat on that one.

26:46.000 --> 26:50.000
And as soon as Coinneach saw you, he was over to get you.

26:50.000 --> 26:53.000
But he wasn’t so keen, well he’d be in no hurry.

26:53.000 --> 26:57.000
That’s good, that’s really good. I like that.

26:55.000 --> 26:57.000
That’s right.

26:58.000 --> 27:00.000
Those dogs are amazing.

27:00.000 --> 27:04.000
Ùisdean told me a story about his grandfather’s dog as well,

27:04.000 --> 27:06.000
um this dog was named Fly.

27:06.000 --> 27:08.000
Fly, seadh.

27:08.000 --> 27:13.000
And the lads were out hunting fowl,

27:13.000 --> 27:16.000
waterfowl and they had their hides set up and…

27:16.000 --> 27:20.000
it was his, I think it was Mary’s grandfather actually,

27:21.000 --> 27:24.000
and he had to get away to catch a boat early in the morning

27:24.000 --> 27:28.000
so before, it was still dark, and he left.

27:28.000 --> 27:33.000
And he went with his dog back to his house in Baleshare

27:33.000 --> 27:36.000
and made ready to leave and catch the boat

27:36.000 --> 27:38.000
and away he was going to the mainland.

27:38.000 --> 27:41.000
And he got back and he realised that, you know,

27:41.000 --> 27:44.000
back then they had the rifles they had to pour

27:44.000 --> 27:47.000
two flasks of powder in the shot.

27:47.000 --> 27:52.000
And he’d forgotten one of his flasks so he said to Fly:

27:52.000 --> 27:54.000
“Right, go and get my flask.”

27:54.000 --> 27:56.000
So the dog was practically out the door

27:56.000 --> 27:59.000
before he’d even opened it, and away Fly went.

27:59.000 --> 28:01.000
It was about a mile and a half walk

28:01.000 --> 28:03.000
or a mile and a half.

28:04.000 --> 28:06.000
And the two men were still there in the hide

28:06.000 --> 28:09.000
and Fly showed up, you know, growling and…

28:09.000 --> 28:11.000
and wanting something and, you know by this time

28:11.000 --> 28:13.000
the two men, they discovered the um,

28:13.000 --> 28:16.000
the other man’s flask, and they said

28:16.000 --> 28:18.000
“Och for goodness’ sake he’s wanting the flask,

28:18.000 --> 28:21.000
give him the flask, he’s going to bite our heads off,”

28:21.000 --> 28:24.000
because Fly is very determined, he’s going to take that flask.

28:24.000 --> 28:26.000
So they gave him the flask and that’s what he wanted

28:26.000 --> 28:29.000
and away he went back home and… and that was it.

28:29.000 --> 28:31.000
That was it? That was it? Oh aye, yes.

28:31.000 --> 28:32.000
Very quick.

28:32.000 --> 28:37.000
Kenny, Kenneth Morrison’s dog, Coinneach [XX],

28:38.000 --> 28:42.000
apparently one day, I heard the story that old Kenneth

28:42.000 --> 28:47.000
was going to the post office, and he was sort of

28:47.000 --> 28:51.000
a grieve on the island then.

28:51.000 --> 28:53.000
What does that mean?

28:53.000 --> 28:57.000
That’s like a, like a farm manager.

28:58.000 --> 29:04.000
Like a farm manager. Em, and he saw a dead sheep.

29:04.000 --> 29:09.000
Now, wool was very pricey in those days, it was worth a lot.

29:09.000 --> 29:12.000
Nowadays, it’s nothing.

29:12.000 --> 29:16.000
And he skinned the sheep. 

29:18.000 --> 29:24.000
Apparently, there was this old lady crossing the hill

29:25.000 --> 29:30.000
a bit later and… unknown to Kenneth,

29:30.000 --> 29:39.000
when he skinned the sheep, he put the skin with the wool on a rock

29:39.000 --> 29:44.000
just to dry and he would pick it up on the way back

29:44.000 --> 29:46.000
from the post office. 

29:47.000 --> 29:53.000
But unknown to him, Clyde lay down beside it

29:53.000 --> 29:56.000
and Kenneth carried on.

29:56.000 --> 29:59.000
And this [tape trips] she saw the skin and she said:

30:00.000 --> 30:03.000
“My Christmases have come in July.”

30:03.000 --> 30:10.000
And she was, started to roll it up and take it with her.

30:11.000 --> 30:16.000
And then, Clyde appeared and he got hold of her skirt

30:17.000 --> 30:20.000
and then she realised the dog.

30:20.000 --> 30:26.000
And the dog started to growl and not only, she let the thing go,

30:28.000 --> 30:30.000
but the dog made her sit down,

30:31.000 --> 30:33.000
everytime she moved, the dog would get

30:33.000 --> 30:37.000
a hold of her skirt until Kenneth came.

30:38.000 --> 30:41.000
The dog meant to give her a red face, sort of thing.

30:41.000 --> 30:44.000
So that was Clyde, that’s the same dog.

30:44.000 --> 30:51.000
Aye, they’re amazing. So amazing… Very good.

30:46.000 --> 30:49.000
They’re amazing. Yes, aye.

30:51.000 --> 30:53.000
Do you know any stories about St Kilda?

30:53.000 --> 30:55.000
Ehh… yes.

30:55.000 --> 30:57.000
I was out there last summer.

30:57.000 --> 30:59.000
Yes, I’ve been out three times.

31:00.000 --> 31:06.000
Now, well, you see the people that’s down here,

31:06.000 --> 31:10.000
all the houses in this part, Borve,

31:10.000 --> 31:13.000
was broken up in 1900.

31:14.000 --> 31:18.000
I think, probably the story goes that

31:18.000 --> 31:22.000
before the men went to the Boer War, 

31:22.000 --> 31:25.000
that is the late 1800s,

31:26.000 --> 31:33.000
they were: ‘right, if you come back safe and sound, you will get land.’

31:35.000 --> 31:41.000
So all those that did come back, when they did come,

31:42.000 --> 31:46.000
there was no word of giving them a plot of land

31:46.000 --> 31:49.000
so they just took the bull by the horns

31:49.000 --> 31:54.000
and put a spade on a shoulder and they walked down here.

31:54.000 --> 31:58.000
And they just put out lots of land for themselves.

31:59.000 --> 32:08.000
And then the factor must have gone back to the landlord and said:

32:08.000 --> 32:12.000
“My good man, these men mean business.”

32:13.000 --> 32:17.000
So that was the end of that.

32:18.000 --> 32:21.000
That was 1900 it was broken up 

32:22.000 --> 32:25.000
and twenty-four crofters came down here.

32:26.000 --> 32:32.000
Em, and before that it was just,

32:32.000 --> 32:35.000
as I said before, it was a tack.

32:36.000 --> 32:42.000
And when they built it the first thing they did was the byre,

32:42.000 --> 32:44.000
and the steading for the cattle.

32:44.000 --> 32:50.000
And they slept, they slept in that byre the first,

32:50.000 --> 32:54.000
for the first summer, like my grandfather and grandmother

32:54.000 --> 33:00.000
and my father was twelve years old when they came down

32:59.000 --> 33:01.000
when they came down from the other end of the island,

33:01.000 --> 33:05.000
Beasdaire, to Borve here.

33:06.000 --> 33:09.000
And they were sleeping in the byre over there 

33:10.000 --> 33:19.000
when this old man from St Kilda, used to come in to communion,

33:20.000 --> 33:25.000
and my grandfather, I think his name was Finlay,

33:25.000 --> 33:30.000
but my grandfather was talking to him and asked:

33:32.000 --> 33:35.000
“How did life meet him?” 

33:35.000 --> 33:39.000
Or how was life since he last saw him,

33:39.000 --> 33:43.000
and he was coming every year, every summer to the communion.

33:44.000 --> 33:48.000
And he said: “Well, they nearly lost me.”

33:49.000 --> 33:52.000
One rule they had in St Kilda was

33:52.000 --> 33:57.000
they weren’t allowed to go wildfowling on their own,

33:57.000 --> 33:59.000
there would have to be two.

33:59.000 --> 34:02.000
And they were the finest climbers in the world.

34:03.000 --> 34:12.000
But up they went and the, they each went to a crag of their own.

34:14.000 --> 34:21.000
Now, this old Finlay, he moored himself on a rock

34:21.000 --> 34:25.000
down a slope and then the sheer face of the rock.

34:26.000 --> 34:30.000
When he had five or six birds,

34:30.000 --> 34:34.000
now the house was getting short of food,

34:34.000 --> 34:38.000
and this was their only source of food, 

34:38.000 --> 34:44.000
and to tell you the priceless…

34:48.000 --> 34:50.000
now I’ll have to rephrase that.

34:50.000 --> 34:55.000
I mean what it was worth to them, I mean,

34:55.000 --> 35:00.000
food was priceless, and they had to go out and get it this way.

35:01.000 --> 35:07.000
When he was, he had six or seven birds, so he was quite happy

35:07.000 --> 35:09.000
and he had a rope around the waist

35:09.000 --> 35:11.000
and he would just stick them in the rope.

35:11.000 --> 35:15.000
And he started to climb up, up the face and

35:15.000 --> 35:20.000
then up this very, very steep slope when the rope broke.

35:21.000 --> 35:24.000
And he just put his back to the rock,

35:24.000 --> 35:26.000
and he could feel himself sliding,

35:26.000 --> 35:30.000
sliding down, and eventually everything went quiet

35:30.000 --> 35:31.000
and he thought he had passed out.

35:31.000 --> 35:36.000
But he said, as he opened his eyes, he was conscious.

35:36.000 --> 35:38.000
And when he looked around him,

35:38.000 --> 35:40.000
he was sitting on the ledge

35:40.000 --> 35:43.000
with his feet dangling with about six,

35:43.000 --> 35:47.000
seven hundred feet to the sea.

35:47.000 --> 35:52.000
And my grandfather said: “What did you do?”

35:52.000 --> 35:55.000
“Oh,” he says, “I started shouting to the other fellow.”

35:55.000 --> 36:00.000
Now this is the reason for them, the law they had,

36:00.000 --> 36:03.000
because St Kilda had a parliament of its own,

36:03.000 --> 36:05.000
this what they called the St Kilda parliament, because

36:05.000 --> 36:09.000
they met every morning to discuss the day’s work.

36:09.000 --> 36:15.000
And the other fellow came and he let him,

36:15.000 --> 36:17.000
let the rope down to him,

36:17.000 --> 36:20.000
and my grandfather – and this is what my father said

36:20.000 --> 36:21.000
that really amused him –

36:21.000 --> 36:26.000
“Now,” he says, my father asked him:

36:26.000 --> 36:30.000
“I suppose you let the birds go?”

36:30.000 --> 36:34.000
“Oh no,” he said, “they were the first to go up.”

36:35.000 --> 36:38.000
Now that shows you the worth, I mean,

36:38.000 --> 36:44.000
food was priceless, and that’s old Finlay,

36:44.000 --> 36:49.000
how near… how nearly he got lost.

36:49.000 --> 36:53.000
So they had a very, very rough time in St Kilda.

36:53.000 --> 36:56.000
Mhm, sin agad e.

36:54.000 --> 36:56.000
Cut! [laughs]